WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.040
Identity is a foundational component of Zero Trust because access decisions need a

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.925
reliable understanding of who or what is making a request.

00:00:07.925 --> 00:00:12.050
Identities include employees, contractors, partners, applications, services,

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.390
and automated workloads.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.160
Authentication establishes evidence about an identity.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.120
Stronger methods can reduce reliance on a single password and provide greater confidence,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.720
but authentication is only one part of the decision.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.020
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity may do.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.140
Zero Trust encourages organizations to connect permissions to job responsibilities,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.190
business need, resource sensitivity,

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:35.340
and current risk rather than granting broad access by default.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.180
Context improves the access decision.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.785
Relevant signals may include the requested application, device status, sign-in pattern,

00:00:41.785 --> 00:00:45.110
location consistency, time, privilege level, data sensitivity,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.645
and whether the request aligns with expected work.

00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:50.185
Identity lifecycle management is equally important.

00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:51.110
Joiners, movers,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.840
and leavers need timely changes so access reflects current responsibilities.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.130
Dormant accounts, outdated group membership,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.020
and unreviewed privileges create unnecessary exposure.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.210
Workload identities deserve the same attention as human users.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.710
Applications, services, scripts, and cloud workloads often access sensitive systems,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.330
and their credentials and permissions need clear ownership, appropriate limits,

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.125
and monitoring.

00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:16.195
Third-party and federated access require governance because the organization may rely on

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:18.060
another party's identity processes.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.685
Contracts, technical controls, review procedures,

00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:23.955
and resource-specific policy help manage that shared responsibility.

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:27.170
Identity-centered security is not identity-only security.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.405
Device posture, resource policy, network and application context, monitoring,

00:01:31.405 --> 00:01:35.360
and data protection still contribute to a complete Zero Trust decision.
